On the project guides, the model correctly read the 1,336,320 additional LoRA parameters and the 120 passages in the language-retention sample. It gave “36” for an annotated score of “36/64”. The strict scorer also rejected some semantically compatible paraphrases. For example, “approximately one week” included the SQuAD reference “one week”. Across all 40 requests, a descriptive whole-phrase check found a normalized reference inside nine completed candidate answers and two Base answers. All nine candidate answers had retrieved reference evidence. The prespecified exact-grounding count remains six.

On an additional installed-CLI request, both arms received the same 180 opening tokens and access to two real project files. The user asked for the command to run the Phase 6 reasoning pair. The trained reader searched TRAINING.md and reported that it could not verify the answer. Its three retrieved passages omitted the reference command, `scripts/run_phase6.sh reasoning-pair`. It finished in 1.58 s. Base exhausted the 384-token allowance in 6.34 s without calling the tool. The archived trace includes both generated streams, the excerpts and the exact file hashes.

== Learning from a calculator's actual rejection
On all ten original arithmetic field requests the installed typed decoder made at least one call that raised an exception. For example, the model supplied an equation including an equals sign to an interface that accepts arithmetic expressions. Retrying the same string consumed another one of the user's three turns. We use execution feedback to train a second recurrent view of the same frozen backbone. CRITIC [55] uses external feedback when revising an answer. TRICE [56] trains models to decide when and how to call tools using execution feedback. Our experiment keeps the opening policy fixed and uses the original exception class as its only new signal at inference. This lets us check the exact preservation of each unaffected request.

The opening decoder acts freely on 256 GSM8K training questions. We save every opening and run its actual proposed arguments through the original validator and callback. Only a genuine callback exception can supply a repair example. The two supervision targets are the publisher-derived, independently executed correct program and the answer after that program returns its value. At least 64 genuine failures are required before fitting. During evaluation, a validated call that raises an exception inside the supplied calculator can activate the new adapter for the remaining turns. It obtains a fresh cache and receives the entire original conversation. We reserve 48 previously unenrolled human word problems, half ASDiv and half SVAMP, and repeat all 72 public and earlier field requests. The primary compares free final answers and successful executions under the same three-turn and 384-token limits for the recurrent model and Base. Exact preservation of unaffected historical outputs is a separate requirement. Fresh word-problem success requires a completed correct answer and an executed arithmetic operation whose observed result equals the reference. We also count correct final numbers independently of tool use. The historical mixed score retains its original endpoint for each request, including final-number accuracy on the ten earlier arithmetic items.

Of 256 free training attempts, 119 produced real calculator exceptions. The 238 resulting supervised turns supplied 90,830 target exposures in 640 updates. Prefix preparation took 28.86 s and optimization took 194.63 s. On all 120 evaluation requests the recovery system achieved 44 strict successes versus 12 for Base (35 gains, three losses, exact $p=6.68 times 10^(-8)$). Total time was 139.63 versus 506.10 seconds. Summed first-token latency was 21.70 versus 15.45 seconds and peak native allocation was 1,810,590,710 versus 1,805,202,394 bytes. All 17 previously successful public calls survived. Two earlier correct field answers were lost. Every recorded historical turn before an exception, and every unaffected request, reproduced its original tokens.

On the 48 fresh human problems, eight recurrent replies were correctly grounded versus zero for Base ($p=0.00781$). Crediting any correct final quantity reversed the result: ten versus 26 (two recurrent-only and eighteen Base-only successes, $p=0.000402$). The recurrent system finished the fresh requests in 59.96 versus 191.76 seconds. It gave seven correct ASDiv quantities versus thirteen, and three correct SVAMP quantities versus thirteen. Recovery activated on 38 fresh questions. On 37 it subsequently obtained a numerical return, but only five ended in a grounded correct answer. In the sit-up problem it replaced the rejected equations with steps `24/3` and `r1*4`, used the returned 32 and answered 32. The observed effect on fresh final answers closed promotion.

