In a separate installed CLI exchange about four crates of eighteen jars with five broken, the learned recovery replaced a rejected equation with two executable expressions and returned 67. On a new question about serving rolls from three trays of fourteen after setting aside eight, its first rejected message contained the correct 34. The repair divided 42 by 8, received 21/4 and reported it. Base answered 34. Both complete exchanges are preserved with their true observations.

== Using the publisher’s function-calling interface

Qwen's published chat template serializes function declarations, assistant actions, typed arguments and observations into distinct parts of the conversation [57]. The earlier tool fits used a project-defined JSON action. To test transfer to the published interface, we train a new pair of rank-32 adapters at two applications of the final layer on the instruction sibling's eight-bit prefix. Each of the 1,244 fitting targets uses the original tokenizer template. The mixture contains 400 examples from the earlier typed curriculum, 480 GSM8K program or answer turns, 160 WikiSQL turns and 204 calls and answers from 51 actual two-request package conversations. Training uses 900 AdamW updates of six turns each. Separate fixed-prefix tensors on disk bound the working memory. Both passes use the same trained parameters.

The comparison includes 22 public executable numerical problems, twelve fresh ASDiv and twelve fresh SVAMP questions, six package-version conversations and six local-file conversations. The twelve project-authored conversations each require two requests over different resources with different correct answers. English, Spanish, German and French wording appears in evaluation. The file functions receive no examples in the 204 service turns. One enrolled distribution, requests, is absent. Its reference is the actual missing-package error, and the scorer accepts its exact text. Each model receives the official schemas and the same opening tokens, greedy thinking and budget of three turns of at most 384 tokens per user request. On a follow-up, each model sees its own previous calls, observations and reply. The unit for the primary paired test is a complete dialogue. Each service exchange requires exactly the reference call, the real observation and a reply containing only the correct numerical quantity or version among its numbers. Correct human arithmetic answers receive separate credit even when the model produced no valid calculator action. Every archived call is checked against the enrolled tool and observation. We also reconstruct the full input sequence and verify the retained position at every physical attention site.

The two-pass fit used 802,816 trainable parameters and 168,464 supervised target exposures. Frozen input preparation took 199.60 s and optimization took 298.70 s. It completed every one of 70 requests and solved 35/58 whole dialogues. Base completed 60 requests and solved 30 dialogues. Twelve paired gains and seven losses gave $p=0.359$. The recurrent model used 4,009 output tokens and 55.44 seconds against 18,840 tokens and 286.35 seconds. Peak allocation fell from 1.69 to 1.11 GiB, while summed time to first token rose from 11.56 to 12.21 seconds. The primary joint threshold was missed.

The registered number-extraction rubric credited eight of twelve native service conversations versus two for Base. An operational recount below gives ten versus nine with exactly one correct call, or ten versus ten allowing a repeated correct call. File pairs were 4/6 versus 0/6. Package pairs were 4/6 versus 2/6. There were six gains and zero losses across these twelve conversations (descriptive exact $p=0.03125$). On the individual service requests, candidate/Base grounded counts were 9/12 versus 1/12 for files and 10/12 versus 7/12 for packages. Both models were timed on the same read-only services. This small result concerns the enrolled paths, distributions and wording.

On the repeated BFCL numerical requests, the native-format loop scored 21/22 and Base scored 20/22. The loop preserved all 17 successes of the original typed checkpoint measured on the compact protocol. The unchanged Base weights had scored 0/22 strict successes under the compact protocol. In these separate measurements the same questions and executable functions yielded substantially different Base behavior under the publisher's interface. The 17-to-zero compact-format comparison alone overstates the practical advantage against a Base installation using its own native template. The native loop took 21.96 seconds for these 22 requests versus 96.86 s for Base. On fresh ASDiv and SVAMP, final numbers favored Base, fifteen versus seven. Grounded arithmetic successes were six for the recurrent model versus eight for Base. These losses and higher first-token latency kept the new adapter opt-in.

