On an ungraded installed query, the native-format loop and Base both read the registered experiment file and returned 3,301 bytes and 35 lines. The loop took 1.57 and 0.80 s, against 4.21 and 1.91 s for Base. On the Hindi NumPy/MLX follow-up, the native-format loop answered in Hindi without calling either package tool or reporting a version. Base called the correct version function for both packages, communicated the NumPy version, then repeated its MLX call through the turn limit. A clean installation of the 592,066-byte runtime wheel outside the checkout reproduced all four recurrent exchanges, including both failures in Hindi. We verified the prompt and generated IDs, actual observations and retained attention histories on every turn. Two fresh wheel builds from the pinned source with a fixed ZIP timestamp had identical SHA-256. All 284 unpacked files also matched the original distributed wheel, and the rebuilt installation reproduced all four exchanges. The earlier compact-format assistant returned a stale version string on the same follow-up.

== Exact verification of model-written equations

We observed the original calculator reject mathematically valid answers written as equalities. After each exception, a learned repair may rewrite both the syntax and the arithmetic. A separate opt-in provider accepts expressions and equations. It parses each side, computes the rational value from the original decimal spelling, resolves references to earlier actual results and requires exact equality before appending a result. An incorrect or undefined side raises a callback error. An ordinary expression returns its computed value as before.

We replayed the original arguments of all 38 fresh requests that activated recovery, without regenerating any answer. The checked provider accepted 32 original programs. Thirteen of their final results matched the target. The remaining nineteen satisfied their own equations and computed the wrong quantity for the question. These are counterfactual provider outputs; the fresh paired comparison below uses actual model replies.

The primary comparison on this interface uses the qualified recurrent tool weights and untouched Base. It enrolls twelve previously unused ASDiv and twelve SVAMP problems with the same frozen questions, schemas, thinking budget, callback implementation and scoring. Earlier developer observations and the fitted error-recovery adapter provide no model weights or test items for this pair. A grounded success requires a computed arithmetic result matching the publisher's quantity and a correct visible final answer. The registered decision also checks per-source final answers, actual equality use, total wall time, summed time to first token and peak allocation.

On 24 fresh publisher questions with the checked calculator, the qualified loop produced nine grounded answers versus zero for Base (nine gains, zero losses, exact $p=0.00391$). It used verified equalities on fifteen questions, six leading to grounded success. Correct final quantities were nine versus ten, with five gains, six losses and $p=1$. On ASDiv the score was 5/12 versus 4/12 and on SVAMP 4/12 versus 6/12. The loop took 30.56 versus 93.89 seconds with peak native allocation 1.08 versus 1.67 GiB. Summed first-token latency was 3.14 versus 2.74 seconds. The field comparison closed model promotion. The compatible optional provider accepts exact checked arithmetic from either model.

In an additional user query about nine boxes of sixteen pencils, with 27 handed out, the loop submitted `9 * 16 = 144` followed by `144 - 27 = 117`. The tool independently verified and returned 144 and 117. Both models answered 117. Base gave its answer without submitting a call. The archived CLI transcript preserves both calculations and generated responses.

== Can the model recognize a successful call that answered the wrong question?

An arithmetic program can run perfectly and still compute the wrong quantity. For example, multiplication of a unit price and a count gives the total cost. Division uses the same inputs to compute a different quantity. The exception-recovery experiment provided feedback only when the executor rejected a call. We therefore trained a new review policy on *successful* executions with different meanings. Cobbe et al. [4] rank generated solutions with a trained verifier. Lightman et al. [58] compare outcome and intermediate-step supervision. Here the same generative decoder must decide whether to accept the returned value or issue another real call.

The initialization was the pinned, unmerged Base text checkpoint distributed in release 05. We quantized the embedding and projections outside the last layer to eight bits in groups of 64. The first action used ordinary depth. After a tool returned, the model applied native layer 23 twice with a stabilized residual and trained rank-32 adapters. Both views shared immutable weight arrays and maintained separate physical attention histories. Every question and follow-up used the same rule, including questions on which the model answered directly. The no-tools path continued to use release 05. Long-context evidence comes from separate studies.

For each of 160 GSM8K and 160 MAWPS training problems we executed a verified calculation and an altered, numerically different calculation using the same problem quantities. The targets taught the model to accept the right return, revise the wrong calculation, and answer after a successful revision. A further 51 pairs used actual installed package versions, with test distributions excluded. We mixed in 128 archived typed-tool response targets. The total was 1,241 teacher-forced turns. Only 802,816 adapter parameters were trainable. We fixed 900 AdamW updates of six examples, seed 924702, and a maximum of 1,800 native seconds before fitting.

The primary comparison used 48 previously unenrolled human arithmetic questions, 22 public BFCL calls and 12 real two-request service conversations. Base used the published Qwen function protocol at full precision and ordinary depth. Both decoders had identical first-request token IDs, reasoning enabled, greedy decoding, 384 tokens per turn and up to three turns per request. We charged cold weight loading, conversion and tokenization to the first request. An independent 24-question SVAMP diagnostic supplied the two models with identical correct and incorrect numerical receipts. After a fixed empty thinking block, we measured the logit difference between starting the returned answer and starting a new call. A question passed only when both margins had the correct sign. This diagnostic measures a binary preference at a fixed prefix. The primary comparison instead requires the model to produce all of its own tokens and observations.

On the matched diagnostic the review decoder favored a new call for all 24 incorrect receipts and favored the returned answer for 16 of 24 correct receipts. Base scored 17 and eight respectively. The loop made both decisions correctly on 16 questions versus two for Base, with 14 paired gains, zero losses and exact $p=0.000122$. Each decision compared two first tokens after the same supplied thinking boundary. It gives no credit for constructing a replacement calculation.

Free generation gave a different result. The two systems completed 29 and 30 strictly correct dialogues out of 82 (six gains, seven losses, $p=1$). On the 48 human questions they gave 11 versus 19 correct final quantities (three gains and eleven losses, $p=0.0574$). The loop grounded six answers and Base grounded eight. The loop completed 21/22 public calls, 2/6 file pairs and 0/6 package pairs. Base completed 20/22, 0/6 and 2/6. Total cold-stream time favored the loop, 242.35 versus 408.50 s. Summed first-token time was 15.59 versus 15.19 s and maximum native allocation was 1.56 versus 1.69 GiB. The release threshold failed.

Eighteen of the loop's 48 arithmetic openings reached their output limit before executing anything. Among the five that began with a legal but numerically wrong result, zero ended in a grounded correction. The salmon question asked how many of 55 families remained after 25 departed. The model divided 25 by 55 and accepted the resulting 5/11. It also reported 84 correctly on a bottle-count question after its last calculation returned 49. Separately scoring the callback and the final number exposes both errors. On 40 of the 82 initial exchanges, both decoders issued the same first call or calls. Four of those exchanges became strict wins and two became losses for the loop. These are descriptive conditional counts because the first action was generated by each model.

